<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
        The ONE network permission, added 2026-09-19 on founder direction:
        "Live rates for FX converter, suggest reliable API and implement. Lets
        fix the data privacy later on."

        It was in the DEBUG manifest only, which is why a live fetch would have
        worked on the founder's emulator and failed silently in a real build.

        Salapify makes exactly one request, from lib/data/fx_service.dart, to
        open.er-api.com, with no key, no account, no identifying header and no
        body. Nothing about the user's money leaves the device.

        THREE THINGS THIS BREAKS, and all three are for before the store
        rather than for today:
          - the app's own header says "Offline Only";
          - main.dart's doc comment says there is no network call anywhere;
          - Play's data safety form has to declare it.
        They are written up in docs/reviews/fx-live-rates.md rather than left
        for somebody to rediscover in a rejection email.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!--
        NOTIFICATIONS, added 2026-09-19 on founder direction: "Yes, build real
        phone notifications now."

        POST_NOTIFICATIONS is a RUNTIME permission from Android 13. Salapify
        does NOT ask for it at launch: the switch lives in Settings, off by
        default, and the dialog appears only when somebody turns it on. The
        Android permission dialog has no second chance, so asking before a
        person has seen what the app does is how an app collects a permanent
        no, and the only way back is a system settings screen nobody finds.

        RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED lets the plugin put its schedule back after the
        phone restarts. Without it, every reminder is silently lost the first
        time somebody reboots, and nothing anywhere says so.

        DELIBERATELY ABSENT: SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM and USE_EXACT_ALARM. Google
        restricts those to alarm clocks and calendars and a budgeting app
        would have to argue for them at review. Salapify schedules INEXACTLY
        (AndroidScheduleMode.inexactAllowWhileIdle), because a bill reminder
        that lands at 9:07 instead of 9:00 is the same reminder.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
    <!--
        NO CLOUD BACKUP OF THE LEDGER. Decided 2026-09-19 on the founder's
        instruction to have the security and privacy reviewer settle it.

        Android's default is allowBackup="true", which quietly copies the app's
        files to the user's Google Drive. The reason that is refused here is
        not the encryption, which is genuinely good: it is that the copy is one
        Salapify cannot see, cannot verify and cannot delete. The honest answer
        to "delete everything you hold about me" has to be one sentence,
        "uninstall the app", and a Drive copy turns it into a treasure hunt
        through Google One settings.

        It also is not only the user's own data. Debt.person is free text
        naming whoever owes them money, and those people consented to nothing.

        THE COST IS REAL AND IS ACCEPTED: with this off and no export built
        yet, a lost phone means the data is gone. That is precisely why the
        export is a launch blocker rather than something Auto Backup is
        allowed to stand in for.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="Salapify"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
        <!--
            flutter_local_notifications' own receivers, which the PLUGIN DOES
            NOT DECLARE FOR YOU. Its bundled manifest carries the permissions
            and nothing else, so leaving these out compiles, runs, and silently
            delivers no scheduled notification at all. Read from the plugin's
            README at the pinned version rather than remembered.

            ScheduledNotificationReceiver is what actually posts a scheduled
            notification when its time arrives.

            ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver puts the whole schedule back
            after a restart. MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED is in the filter for the same
            reason: an app update wipes pending alarms too, and without it
            every reminder would quietly vanish on the update that was meant
            to improve them.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
